@@ -38,3 +38,37 @@ resolvePointer(config, "");                // the whole document
 Only the JSON string form of a pointer is accepted. A pointer taken from a URI fragment (`#/foo`) must
 be percent-decoded and stripped of its `#` first. See
 [ADR 0001](docs/adr/0001-json-pointer-resolution.md) for the choices made where the RFC leaves room.
+
+## JSON Patch (RFC 6902)
+
+```ts
+import { applyPatch } from "json-toolkit";
+
+const config = { servers: [{ host: "a.example" }], debug: true };
+
+applyPatch(config, [
+  { op: "test", path: "/debug", value: true },
+  { op: "add", path: "/servers/-", value: { host: "b.example" } },
+  { op: "replace", path: "/debug", value: false },
+]);
+// { servers: [{ host: "a.example" }, { host: "b.example" }], debug: false }
+```
+
+- `applyPatch(document, patch)` applies the operations `add`, `remove`, `replace`, `move`, `copy` and
+  `test` in order and returns the patched document as a new value. Neither `document` nor `patch` is
+  modified, and the result shares no value with them, so a patch that fails leaves nothing
+  half-applied.
+- It throws an `InvalidPatchError` when the patch does not follow the RFC: it is not an array, an
+  operation is not an object, its `op` is unknown, a required `path`, `from` or `value` member is
+  missing, a pointer is not valid JSON Pointer syntax, a `value` is not a JSON value, or a `move` would
+  put a value inside itself. The whole patch is checked before any operation runs.
+- It throws a `PatchConflictError` when an operation does not fit the document: its target (or the
+  parent of an `add` target) does not exist, an array index is out of range, or the whole document is
+  removed. In an array, `-` names the position after the last element, for `add`, `move` and `copy`.
+- It throws a `PatchTestFailedError` when a `test` operation finds a different value. Values are
+  compared as JSON: numbers by value, object members regardless of order.
+- All three extend `JsonPatchError`, whose `index` and `operation` name the failing operation. When the
+  error comes from resolving a pointer, the `JsonPointerError` is its `cause`.
+
+Members of an operation other than those the RFC defines are ignored. See
+[ADR 0002](docs/adr/0002-json-patch.md) for the choices made where the RFC leaves room.
