@@ -0,0 +1,37 @@
+# ADR 0002: JSON Patch
+
+- Version: 1
+- Date: 2026-10-21
+- Status: accepted
+
+## Context
+
+`applyPatch` implements RFC 6902 on top of the JSON Pointer resolution of
+[ADR 0001](0001-json-pointer-resolution.md). Our services apply patches received from other systems to
+stored documents, so a failing patch must never leave a document partly changed. The RFC leaves error
+handling to the application (section 5) and some cases open.
+
+## Decisions
+
+1. **The input is never mutated.** The document is deep-copied once, the operations are applied to the
+   copy, and every value taken from the patch is copied too. This makes a patch atomic, as section 5
+   requires, without an undo log, at the cost of one copy of the document per call.
+2. **The whole patch is validated first.** A malformed operation anywhere in the patch is reported as
+   an `InvalidPatchError` even when an earlier operation would fail against the document: a malformed
+   request is a different problem, for the caller, from a document that does not match it.
+3. **Three error classes under a common base.** `InvalidPatchError` (the patch is wrong),
+   `PatchConflictError` (the document does not fit the operation) and `PatchTestFailedError` (a `test`
+   failed) extend `JsonPatchError`, which names the failing operation by its index. This maps onto the
+   HTTP statuses of RFC 5789 (400, 409 and 409 or 412) and lets a caller catch all of them at once.
+4. **Removing the whole document is a conflict.** The RFC does not say what `remove` of `""` means;
+   a document without a value is not a JSON document.
+5. **A `move` into its own child is invalid**, as section 4.4 requires. The check compares reference
+   tokens, so `/a` may move into `/ab/c`. A `move` to the same location is a no-op once `from` is known
+   to exist.
+6. **Values must be JSON.** `value` members holding `undefined`, `NaN`, `Infinity`, functions or
+   class instances such as `Date` are rejected, since patches may be built in code rather than parsed.
+7. **Own members and safe writes.** Members are found with `Object.hasOwn` (ADR 0001, decision 4) and
+   written with `Object.defineProperty`, so a patch adding `/__proto__` creates an ordinary member and
+   cannot change an object's prototype.
+8. **Duplicate members are not detected.** Appendix A.13 shows an operation with two `op` members;
+   once parsed by `JSON.parse` only the last remains, so `applyPatch` cannot see the duplicate.
